\ core::bool -- the typed branch, over the two control primitives it imports
\ and nothing else, so a reader can see this module's whole intrinsic surface
\ in one line.
import: intrinsics i | branch tag | ;

export: if unless ;

\ `if` reads its `bool`'s discriminant and branches on it. Both quotations are
\ `~` (inline-only) and share the input row `..a` and the output row `..b`, so
\ a branch may change the stack shape as long as both agree; the shape change
\ is splice-local and reconciled by the branch's own join.
\
\ The locals are spelled out rather than abbreviated because this body is
\ checked against the whole program's word environment: a local may not shadow
\ a callable name, so a one-letter local here would collide with any program
\ that happens to define a word `t`. They are also prefixed `if--`/`unless--`
\ rather than left as plain `cond`/`then-arm`/`else-arm`: `cond` is DESIGN.md's
\ documented future multi-way branch word, so a bare `cond` local here would
\ collide with the very word this file is meant to grow. (The underlying gap
\ -- library-word locals have no hygiene against user or future stdlib names
\ at all -- is not fixed by this; it is a separate slice.)
\
\ Its tail term is `branch`, whose tail-called parameters are both quotations,
\ so a caller's `... ~[ base ] ~[ ... rec ] if` self-tails through it and lowers
\ to a loop back-edge rather than recursion.
: if inline ( ..a bool ~[ ..a -- ..b ] ~[ ..a -- ..b ] -- ..b )
  | if--else-arm | | if--then-arm | | if--cond |
  if--cond tag if--then-arm if--else-arm branch ;

\ `unless` is `if` with the arms swapped: the first quotation runs when the
\ condition is false.
: unless inline ( ..a bool ~[ ..a -- ..b ] ~[ ..a -- ..b ] -- ..b )
  | unless--else-arm | | unless--then-arm | | unless--cond |
  unless--cond tag unless--else-arm unless--then-arm branch ;
